a41e851ccce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069701 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069702 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069702 #edb/ref 1061 22847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e5127b1-8f72-5af1-9af8-0a68620f1c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069703 #edb/ref 1061 22848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2c8d513-b9f7-5e4d-b550-a588fb3a8ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069704 #edb/ref 1061 22849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d80f89b1-82f3-5c21-ae3e-13d7fbef2b51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069705 #edb/ref 1061 22850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a64fa200-0d99-5bad-916e-7cbe9afb1166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1061 26723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20d6dd44-7fc6-52a1-a076-27ddb6da9696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1061 4295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e24a880-f9da-5e1e-b0bf-5bb2128d72a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1061 4296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eea06921-8121-51a7-86ad-15824cc00550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1061 4299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7015e37-4cd2-5ffb-814e-264ca9a0b345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1065 1605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069979 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by trial and error, which involves substituting a number for the variable and checking to see if we get a true statement.\n\nFor example, which of the following values of $y$ satisfy the inequality $y - 1 > 3\\,?$\n\nI. $y=6$\n\nII. $y=2$\n\nIII. $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 6-1 & >3 \\\\ 5 & >3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **true** statement, so $y=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 2-1 & >3 \\\\ 1 & >3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given equation, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & >3 \\\\ 3-1 & >3 \\\\ 2 & >3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=3$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I satisfies the inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069979 #edb/ref 1065 3879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069979 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c8259a9f-33bd-5c3f-9005-695f9474fbab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069979 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1064 "When an equation contains an $x^2$ term and a constant, it can be solved using the **square root method**. The square root method makes use of the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\vert x \\vert \\,.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, we can solve the equation $2x^2-18=0$ using the following three steps:\n\n1\\. Isolate the $x^2$ on one side of the equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Take the square root of both sides of the equation\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & =3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n3\\. Solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-3\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\nSo, there are two solutions, $x=3$ and $x=-3.$ We can write this more concisely as $x= \\pm 3,$ where the \"$\\pm$\" symbol stands for \"plus or minus.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1065 8692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ef92091-e041-5715-ac4e-75fa146c4cea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070019 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{ or } \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =4-1 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+1) & =4 \\\\ -x-1 & =4 \\\\ -x-1+1 & =4+1 \\\\ -x & =5 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1065 8821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35d16431-4611-59fc-8888-c1db1a7b9902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we've seen that the solutions to the equation \n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nare $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$ \n\nWe can verify that these solutions are correct by substituting them back into the original equation and checking we get a true statement:\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{blue}3}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |3+1| & =4 \\\\ |4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{red}-5}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |-5+1| & =4 \\\\ |-4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{red}-5}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}$ are both solutions to our absolute value equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1065 8822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "561ffe88-9340-5e0a-840a-6946f446a9f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1067 "Checking the Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Expressions Containing Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073788 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a31c2b6-585a-5715-98f8-4b82605f4c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "622cd109-3764-5f32-8205-046be4b57c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Square Root Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073806 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2d8a8280-5248-57e4-a5ed-14e281f3db77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1045 "Adding and Subtracting Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073921 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "17691de4-8d73-56a0-9388-fcd801f22dd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1061 36487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74aa14ec-4817-573b-816c-aa9e7cef4458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1061 36491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b782d57f-6faa-597a-9408-0b6fc960adfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1061 36619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d050b8f3-0cc1-593c-8200-076f5b4f824f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1061 36620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fded71-c2d5-5d66-9f13-5b0253efd6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074728 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1061 4327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38f3ace0-f394-598e-8dad-37fefda9d86e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1061 4328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "459089fc-d278-5487-bc0b-e4cd6237c142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1065 1609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9aa49408-30d5-57b5-8dff-ec1744f9fc6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074948 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers. \n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1065 5995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4af2af2e-0f81-5094-adf6-2ede72a493d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075012 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{2}-6\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (4-6)\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1065 5996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32078f96-6eaf-57d2-bc71-8af8495d5d6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075013 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions With Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186076286 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "77ddb2f4-a245-56c0-8428-a28384aa5396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1039 "Clearing a Rational Expression With a Variable on One Side" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078577 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 175921860781